# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Clock {
  static func monotonic() -> Int64
  static func realtime() -> Int64
  static var timingIsReal: Bool { get }
}

enum ThreadSlot {
  static var value: UInt { get set }
}

func sleep(until deadline: Int64)
```

# DESCRIPTION

`Clock.monotonic` returns nanoseconds on the monotonic clock.
It is the time base of every deadline in Sys: the `deadline` arguments of `Handle.wait`, `Port.wait`, `Channel.call`, `Futex.wait` and `Counter.wait` are values of this clock, and `infiniteDeadline` is a deadline that never passes.

`Clock.realtime` returns nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, for timestamps that people read.
It follows UTC once the time service has published a UTC page; until then it returns the monotonic clock.

`Clock.timingIsReal` is whether durations mean anything: true on hardware, under KVM and hosted, false under QEMU's TCG, whose clock runs at the emulation's pace (amd64 tells TCG by its CPUID leaf; arm64 and rv64 say false, as they run only under TCG).
A measurement that would take long under TCG is made only where it is true.

`sleep(until:)` suspends the calling thread until `deadline`.
It is a wait on the thread's own port with a deadline and nothing bound to it.
It has no result.

`ThreadSlot.value` is one word that belongs to the calling thread.
A runtime keeps its per-thread record there; the C library keeps its thread record in it.
It reads as 0 until set.
It is stored in the thread's block, which the thread pointer addresses.

